  "response": "Distinguishing a tornado from a downburst after the fact rests chiefly on the geometry of the wreckage, because wind leaves a directional signature in whatever it moves. Convergent or rotational debris patterns indicate tornadic winds: trees, roofing, and structural members are thrown inward toward a damage centerline or twisted into curving arcs that record a circulating flow. By contrast, divergent debris patterns fanning outward from a central point indicate a downburst, in which a column of descending air strikes the surface and spreads radially. Whereas tornadic winds span an enormous range of intensities, downburst winds cannot exceed 60 miles per hour, so surveyors read the severity of the destruction alongside its orientation when assigning a cause. The pattern of the debris, more than any single fallen tree, is therefore the primary evidence a survey team weighs.\n\nThe difficulty arises when the human and instrumental records diverge, as they did in the storm over Millbrook. Although residents of Millbrook reported seeing a funnel cloud during the storm, Doppler radar showed no rotational velocity couplet over Millbrook during the storm. That absence of a couplet carries particular weight. Doppler radar directly measures wind speeds at ground level, and the instrument registered no surface rotation where the witnesses placed one. Survey teams cannot simply defer to the instrument or to the observers in such a case; they must go to the site and examine what the wind actually did to the structures and vegetation it struck.\n\nAt Millbrook, the physical record was unambiguous. The debris at the Millbrook site lay in a parallel pattern pointing in a single direction; in other words, all of the debris at the Millbrook site fell in the same direction. Such uniformity is inconsistent with the inward or curving deposition that a rotating vortex produces, and it shifted the analysis decisively toward a linear wind event. The classification question admits only two outcomes: either the final survey classified the Millbrook damage as a tornado, or it classified the Millbrook damage as non-tornadic, and the two cannot both be true. The National Weather Service survey team settled the question when it formally determined that the Millbrook damage was non-tornadic rather than a tornado. Neighboring locations can complicate such judgments, since the same parent storm may generate more than one damaging mechanism within a few miles. At the farm east of Millbrook, at least one of the following occurred, perhaps both: a rear-flank downdraft struck the farm east of Millbrook, or a brief tornado touched down at the farm east of Millbrook. Rear-flank downdrafts often accompany tornadogenesis, which is why damage at a single property can reflect one process, the other, or a sequence of both.\n\nThe conflict between the eyewitness reports and the radar data required a formal explanation, and it was resolved when the review board determined that the funnel reported by Millbrook residents was a scud cloud, a ragged, low-hanging fragment of condensation that frequently mimics a funnel but has no rotation reaching the ground. With that determination, the residents' observation no longer stood against the radar or the debris evidence. Both the non-tornadic classification and the scud-cloud determination resulted from the fact that the National Weather Service final survey concluded that the Millbrook damage was caused by straight-line downburst winds; once that conclusion was reached, the classification and the reinterpretation of the witness accounts followed from it.\n\nThe procedural record of such a case is as important as the meteorological reasoning, because later researchers and insurers depend on it. Survey teams log damage points using the Damage Assessment Toolkit before survey results are entered into the Storm Data publication. The Toolkit preserves geolocated photographs, estimated wind speeds, and the damage indicators observed at each point, which allows reviewers to reconstruct the debris orientation that drove the final judgment. In contested events, it is this documented spatial evidence, rather than testimony or radar alone, that ultimately determines whether destruction is attributed to a tornado or to a downburst.",
